  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038412/assets/1fa98148cf4302a5524ade655ee09a3f74d248ba766849f62a71f3ec85e860c6.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the surface shown above in spherical polar coordinates?",
  "worked_solution": "In the picture, we are given a half-plane that passes through the $z$-axis.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,\u03d5),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\u03d5\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nEvery point on the given plane makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis. On the other hand, $\\rho $ and $\u03d5$ can vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$",
